"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms=globalThis.webpackChunklearnalgorithms||[]).push([[8637],{2964(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"tag":{"label":"Recursion","permalink":"/concepts/tags/recursion","allTagsPath":"/concepts/tags","count":3,"items":[{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer","title":"Divide and Conquer","description":"Divide and conquer is one of the oldest and most powerful strategies for designing algorithms. Instead of attacking a problem as a whole, it breaks it into smaller problems of the same kind, solves them (usually by applying the same strategy again), and combines their solutions. We have already met two examples: binary search, which discards half of the array at each step, and Quicksort, which partitions the array and sorts each part.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer"},{"id":"algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-examples-1","title":"Divide and Conquer: Numbers and Searches","description":"This lesson applies the paradigm and its analysis to a first set of classic problems: the factorial, sequential and binary search, the Fibonacci numbers and the greatest common divisor. Some of them show the strength of divide and conquer; others, especially Fibonacci, show what happens when it is applied badly. All of them are implemented, with tests, in the course repository.","permalink":"/concepts/algorithm-design-paradigms/divide-and-conquer-examples-1"},{"id":"fundamental-problem-domains/quicksort","title":"Quicksort","description":"Quicksort, invented by Tony Hoare around 1960, is one of the most important algorithms ever designed. It is the basis of the sorting routines of many programming languages and libraries, and it is usually the fastest general-purpose sorting algorithm in practice. It improves the idea of exchange: instead of exchanging adjacent elements, it exchanges elements that are far apart, removing many inversions at once.","permalink":"/concepts/fundamental-problem-domains/quicksort"}],"unlisted":false}}')}}]);